      },
      {
        q:       'Why does find_vendor return found: false instead of raising an error for an unknown supplier?',
        options: ['An unknown vendor is a fact the agent should act on (reject), not a mistake in the call', 'Errors cannot be sent back to the model', 'Raising errors would end the loop', 'Pydantic requires it'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Errors are for wrong calls the model should fix; "not found" is a valid answer.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Why may the agent send any invoice to review, but reject only for a confirmed reason?',
        options: ['Review means a person decides (low cost if wrong); a wrong reject means a supplier who should be paid isn’t', 'Reject is slower', 'Review never happens in practice', 'The model is not allowed to reject'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The gate is strictest where a mistake is most costly or hardest to undo.',
      },
      {
        q:       'What does a three-way match compare?',
        options: ['The invoice, the purchase order and the goods receipt', 'Three invoices from the same vendor', 'The invoice total, tax and currency', 'The vendor, the bank and the contract'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Billed vs. ordered (quantity, price) vs. actually received.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="own exercise, building on lesson 4.4's other-domains reflection and lesson 4.5's agent harness"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Let the agent find the path (adapts to new cases)', b: 'Branching workflow (predictable, fewer LLM calls)' },
      { a: 'Strict auto_pay gate (safe)', b: 'More invoices land on people’s desks' },
      { a: 'Prompt telling the model to ignore instructions', b: 'Gates in code that make it irrelevant' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'The gate checks tool results, but the tool inputs (invoice lines, bank digits) come from the model. How could an attacker exploit that, and how would you close the gap?',
        hint:   'What if the model reports the account digits from the old invoice instead of the new one?',
        answer: '<p>An injection could try to get the model to report different values to the tools, for example the account digits on file instead of the ones on the invoice, so verify_bank_details matches. Defenses: extract the payment-critical fields with a separate, narrow extraction call (or a regex) that never sees the free-text notes; prefer structured e-invoices; have the gate re-read critical fields from the source document itself; and require a person to approve any change of bank details, whatever the agent concludes.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'Finance wants invoices under $500 without a PO paid automatically. What changes, and what could go wrong?',
        hint:   'Gate, prompt, and what a fraudster would do next.',
        answer: '<p>The gate gets a new auto_pay condition (no PO, amount under the limit, vendor and bank checks passed), and the prompt describes it. The risk is invoice splitting: a fraudster or careless vendor sends several $490 invoices. Add a check of the vendor’s total over a period, and monitor no-PO payments just under the limit.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Trusting the prompt alone to stop prompt injection.',
      'Letting the agent reject or pay without a tool-confirmed reason.',
      'Treating "not found" as an error, so the agent retries instead of acting on it.',
      'Paying the invoice total instead of the matched total.',
      'Not recording the path, so detours and skipped checks go unnoticed.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>
